+# Formspree email, agreements and Google Calendar
+
+## Existing delivery
+
+The owner has confirmed that Formspree emails are arriving. The existing endpoint remains unchanged. Development tests intercept Formspree requests and never send real inquiries or agreements.
+
+## Client acknowledgements — how to configure them
+
+In Formspree, open your form, choose **Workflow → Actions → + Add New → Auto Response**, then set the sender name, subject and message. Formspree currently documents this feature for Professional and Business plans, and it requires the submitted `email` field, which both site forms already provide. [1](https://help.formspree.io/hc/en-us/articles/360025007233-Sending-a-confirmation-or-response-email)
+
+Both forms currently share one Formspree endpoint, so use an acknowledgement suitable for either type of submission, for example:
+
+> Thank you for contacting OJ_Oyesola. Your submission has been received, and I will review it and respond within 24 hours. A requested date is not reserved automatically; availability and booking confirmation are handled separately.
+
+Do not use an automatic “your booking is confirmed” message for every inquiry. If you later want distinct automatic booking and agreement responses, use separate forms/workflows and wire their endpoint IDs accordingly.
+
+Without an autoresponse feature, you can reply manually to the notification: Formspree uses a form field named `email` as the Reply-To address and documents this on all plans. [3](https://help.formspree.io/hc/en-us/articles/360012262774-Email-Reply-To-address)
+
+Send a test from an email account you control, then check both the photographer's inbox and the submitting account's inbox/spam folder. Confirm the Reply-To address and that the wording does not promise a reserved date. This should be done by the owner; the test suite does not perform real email sends.
+
+## What is now included in your email
+
+All new submissions include:
+
+- The submitted form values, including intended/agreed image usage.
+- `submitted_at_utc` and a submission-type label.
+- A `google_calendar_link` when a valid date is provided.
+
+Signed agreements also include:
+
+- The typed signature and checked consent value.
+- The agreement version.
+- A text snapshot of the terms displayed on the signed page.
+
+Keep the received email as the record. The browser timestamp is client-supplied; the Formspree/email receipt time is the received-submission timestamp. This is recordkeeping, not a claim of independently verified identity or a qualified e-signature service.
+
+To organise Gmail, open a received notification, use its menu to **Filter messages like these**, and create labels such as **Website enquiries** and **Signed agreements** using the actual sender/subject shown in your inbox. Review your retention and backup settings; a labelled email is not an independent backup.
+
+## Review and add a date to Google Calendar
+
+1. Open the Formspree email and click its `google_calendar_link`.
+2. Sign into your own Google account if needed.
+3. Review the date, choose your business calendar, and set the session's start/end times.
+4. Save only when you want that entry on your calendar.
+
+The draft title begins **“To confirm:”**. Dates are initially **all-day**, because the forms currently collect a date rather than a confirmed appointment time. The timezone is `Africa/Lagos` in `assets/js/config.js`.
+
+**The website does not create an event, reserve a slot, check availability or request access to your Google account.** Opening a draft passes the name, email, session type and requested date in the link to Google. Agreement text and consent records remain in the Formspree email; they are not copied into the calendar description.
+
+When no date is supplied, the email contains no calendar link. Changing the email's date later does not edit an existing calendar entry; review it in Calendar yourself.
+
+## SEO and sharing
+
+The homepage description and share-description use exactly:
+
+> Creating timeless images, seeing you better...
+
+The share image is the existing About portrait at `About Page/IMG_9627BW.jpg`, not the old graphic. Canonical and Open Graph URL tags are generated into the HTML, so link crawlers do not need JavaScript.
+
+- On the managed host, set `OJ_SITE_URL` to the real HTTPS root domain. New named collections get URLs such as `/crimson/` and appear in the sitemap automatically when published.
+- The static export uses named files such as `crimson.html` so it continues to work on GitHub Pages. Edit `siteUrl` in `assets/js/config.js` and run `python3 tools/build_galleries.py` when changing that static site's domain.
+- Gallery cards support copy-link/new-tab navigation and an **Open shareable page** link in the viewer.
+- Social apps may cache a previous preview and crop portrait thumbnails to their own layouts. The HTML now points directly to the About picture, but each platform controls refresh timing and display crop.
+
+Google Search Console, Google Business Profile, font-licence confirmation, package pricing and testimonial review are left for later as requested.
